Hyundai Motor Company has led the charge in Korea’s auto industry since 1975. Over the years, the brand has grown into a global powerhouse, with its cars now reaching over 200 countries around the world.

In fact, a recent Statista survey ranked Hyundai as the [fourth-largest player in the global automobile market](https://statista.com/statistics/316786/global-market-share-of-the-leading-automakers/).

Still, like all major automakers, Hyundai has had its fair share of recalls - but the brand is typically quick to address issues. If your car is involved in a recall, you’ll receive a letter explaining the problem, potential risks, and the steps needed to resolve it.

As trusted [Hyundai lemon law attorneys](/), we’re here to break down the latest Hyundai car recalls, the specific models affected, and how to protect yourself.

## The Science Behind Recalls

Generally,[car recalls](/blog/top-10-automakers-with-the-most-car-recalls-in-2023) happen when manufacturers discover a problem that impacts the safety and performance of the vehicle, such as:

- Faulty airbags, seatbelts, brakes, or steering

- Malfunctioning engines, transmissions, or other key components

- Wiring issues

- Software glitches

Recalls aim to fix these issues before they lead to accidents or injuries. However, sometimes the problem doesn’t go away after repairs. In these cases, the vehicle may qualify as a “lemon” - a car with persistent defects that affect its value or usability.

If your car is recalled, the manufacturer will notify you and provide details on how to fix the issue, usually for free. It’s best to take action quickly, even for seemingly insignificant problems, to keep yourself and others safe on the road.

## Hyundai Car Recalls in 2024

In 2024, Hyundai announced a series of recalls across several of its popular models. Join us as we break them down.

## 2024 Hyundai Elantra, Kona, and Venue: Loss of Drive Power

Models Affected:

- 2024 Hyundai Elantra

- 2024 Hyundai Kona

- 2024 Hyundai Venue

Drive Power Loss:

In May 2024, Hyundai recalled 13,511 of its 2024 Elantra, Kona, and Venue models. A [report](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2024/RCLRPT-24V308-1849.PDF) from the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) revealed that an electrical short circuit in the Exhaust Gas Recirculation (EGR) valve assembly can cause a sudden loss of drive power, greatly increasing the risk of a crash.

To resolve the issue, Hyundai is offering to replace the EGR valve free of charge. If you own one of the affected vehicles, contact your local Hyundai dealership as soon as possible.

## 2024 Hyundai Santa Fe and 2024 Hyundai Santa Fe Hybrid: Airbag Malfunctions

Models Affected:

- 2024 Hyundai Santa Fe

- 2024 Hyundai Santa Fe Hybrid

Airbag Deployment Issues:

Hyundai also issued a recall for [nearly 50,000](https://www.foxbusiness.com/markets/hyundai-recalls-50k-suvs-us-issue-could-affect-air-bag) of its 2024 Santa Fe and Santa Fe Hybrid SUVs due to concerns over faulty airbags. According to reports, the airbags may either fail to deploy during a crash or, in some cases, deploy unexpectedly without any impact.

The problem first came to light in April 2024 when a rear-seat side airbag in a Santa Fe SUV was deployed without warning in South Korea. Hyundai’s investigation found that the main floor wiring harnesses were rubbing against the second-row passenger bench seat, causing damage that could trigger airbag malfunctions.

So far, there have been eight reported incidents of unexpected airbag deployment, but fortunately, no crashes or injuries have occurred.

## 2024 Hyundai Genesis GV70: Malfunctioning Transmission Harness

Models Affected:

- 2024 Hyundai Genesis GV70

Transmission Control Problems:

More than [15,000 units of the 2024 Hyundai Genesis GV70](https://www.nj.com/business/2024/04/car-recalls-2024-see-the-full-list-of-612k-hyundai-kia-mercedes-benz-and-chrysler-vehicles-recalled.html) were flagged for a problem with the transmission control harness. A water leak can cause a short circuit and shift the transmission from Park to Neutral unexpectedly. If this happens, the car may roll away.

Hyundai has urged Genesis GV70 owners to turn off the ignition and use the emergency brake when parking. Dealers will either repair the connector or replace the harness and internal wiring for free.

However, if your vehicle continues to have issues after multiple repair attempts, you might have a valid claim under [Hyundai lemon law](/manufacturers/hyundai). Contact one of our lemon law attorneys in California to discuss your options.

## 2024 Hyundai Genesis: Fire Hazards

Models Affected:

- 2015-2016 Hyundai Genesis

- 2017-2019 Hyundai Genesis G80

- 2017-2019 Hyundai Genesis G90

- 2019 Hyundai Genesis G70

Engine Compartment Fire Risks:

In February, Hyundai discovered a serious issue in [90,000+ Genesis models](https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/hyundai-recalls-more-than-90-000-genesis-vehicles-in-us-due-to-fire-risk-124021601295_1.html). Water in the starter solenoid can potentially cause an electric short and lead to an engine fire.

As per reports, Hyundai has confirmed 12 “thermal incidents” involving starters in the U.S. between September 2017 and July 2023. Thermal incidents usually involve smoke, melting parts, or fires, although it’s unclear if any actual fires occurred.

The reports also confirmed there were no crashes or injuries related to these incidents.

Hyundai is offering to install a free remedy recall kit to fix the issue. In the meantime, owners have been advised to park their vehicles outside and away from structures.

## What to Do If Your Hyundai Vehicle is Under Recall?

Do you suspect your vehicle is part of a Hyundai car recall? Here’s how to verify.

## Visit Hyundai’s Recall Website

Enter your vehicle’s VIN (Vehicle Identification Number) on Hyundai’s official recall website to see if your car is part of any recall. You’ll find your VIN on the dashboard near the windshield or your registration documents.

## Use the NHTSA Website

The NHTSA also offers a recall lookup tool. Enter your VIN to check for any recalls.

## Look Out for Recall Notices

Hyundai typically sends notices to owners of affected vehicles. Keep an eye on your mail or email for any updates.

## Steps to Take

If your Hyundai car is under recall:

1. Schedule an Appointment

Contact your Hyundai dealership to schedule a repair. Recall fixes are done at no cost to you.

2. Follow Precautionary Measures

Depending on the recall, Hyundai may advise you to avoid driving your car until the repair is completed. Follow these precautions to keep yourself and others safe.

3. Keep All Documentation

Save all paperwork related to the recall and repairs. This may be important later, especially if you need to pursue a lemon claim.

## Your Rights as a Hyundai Car Owner in California

Not all recalled cars can be fully fixed. Some may have serious manufacturing defects that persist even after several repair attempts.

However, as a Hyundai owner in California, you’re protected by the state’s lemon law. A skilled lemon law attorney can negotiate with manufacturers and push for a replacement or refund.

Your Hyundai may qualify as a lemon if:

- It has a significant defect that impacts its safety, value, or usability

- The manufacturer has made multiple unsuccessful repair attempts- typically four or more

- The vehicle has been out of service for 30 or more days (in total) due to repairs

- The vehicle is still under warranty

Under Hyundai lemon law, you may be entitled to various types of compensation for your defective vehicle, including:

- A full refund of the purchase price

- Reimbursement for any incidental costs

- A replacement vehicle, if agreed upon

- Attorney and court costs

While California does have some of the most user-friendly lemon laws in the nation, it’s still important to have an experienced attorney by your side. Manufacturers often try to downplay or even deny lemon law claims. You’ll need proper legal representation to stand up to them and fight for the compensation you deserve.

